<Project>

  <!--
    Every custom MSBuild property in this repo lives here, at the root.

    Godot regenerates src/Dlo.Game/Dlo.Game.csproj. Project references survive that;
    custom properties do not (arch §1.4). So this file is the only durable place to
    put them, and Dlo.Game.csproj is never hand-authored.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      net10.0 overrides the net8.0 Godot generates. This is a decision, not an
      oversight - read arch §1.4 before "fixing" it.

      Godot 4.7.2's project generator hardcodes net8.0 and GodotSharp ships against
      it, but that is Godot's floor, not a ceiling. Measured on 2026-08-24, all four
      legs separately: net10.0 builds against GodotSharp; Godot's own in-editor
      build path (the build-solutions switch) builds it; the editor does not rewrite
      the TargetFramework setting; and it runs, reporting .NET 10.0.11.

      The runtime is .NET 10 at either TFM, because Godot's host reads
      GodotPlugins.runtimeconfig.json (8.0.0 with rollForward: LatestMajor) and takes
      the newest major installed. So net8.0 would only mean compiling against an older
      BCL than the one actually executing - and one that leaves support in November
      2026, before this project ships. There is no runtime cost either way; the only
      thing the TFM buys or denies is compile surface.

      Export is the one leg still unverified - it needs 4.7.2 export templates, which
      are not installed. E18-01 verifies it, and is where the finding goes if it fails.

      This file is the authority for every project that is NOT a Godot project. There are
      two that are - src/Dlo.Game and tests/Dlo.Game.Tests, each with a project.godot beside
      it - and both carry their own explicit net10.0. They have to, and it has to be - first
      measured during E14-03, then watched happening again during E14-05, when the GdUnit4
      adapter launched the editor against the test project and that csproj grew a net8.0 line
      mid-run, in CRLF:

        - MSBuild imports this file *before* the project body, so a TargetFramework
          line inside a .csproj beats this one.
        - Godot re-inserts that line as net8.0 whenever it finds it missing. Deleting
          it does not work; it comes back on the next editor run.
        - Godot does not touch the line once it has a value. It only fills in an
          absent one.

      So each Godot project's csproj carries an explicit net10.0 of its own, and that is what
      makes the override stick, and any future Godot project here needs the same line.
      Arch §1.4's "the editor does not rewrite <TargetFramework>" is accurate but only tells
      half the story - it does not rewrite a value, and it does re-add a missing line.
    -->
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>

    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Warnings-as-errors in Dlo.Domain only (arch §2, standards §1). The Game layer
    compiles Godot's noisy generated glue, and failing the build on it means nobody
    can build. The enforced gate there is the suite instead.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'Dlo.Domain'">
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
